          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Just as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %em closed functor
            is a homomorphism between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+categories"} closed categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functors"} monoidal functors
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong strict closed functor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+categories"} closed categories
            that preserves all the structure on the nose. In particular, it must preserve the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+homs"} internal homs
            and the unit object:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi D
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(I_C) = I_D ,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F([X,Y]) = [F(X),F(Y)].
          %p
            but it should also preserve all of the additional structure transformations. Strict closed functors are not common “in nature” (since their definition violates the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            studies of closed categories, as for example in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem"} coherence problems
          %p
            More commonly occurring in nature are functors that preserve the closed structure merely up to a transformation, which may perhaps be invertible. Just as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functors"} lax monoidal functors
            are often called simply “monoidal functors,” the “lax” sort of closed functor for which the transformation is not invertible are often called simply
            = succeed "," do
              %em closed functors
            = succeed "." do
              %em strong
          %p
            To be precise: a
            %strong (lax) closed functor
            between closed categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\to D
            together with:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} transformation
                = succeed "," do
